    <p class="lic-eyebrow">Licence</p>
    <h1>What you may do with these lessons</h1>
    <p>
      The lesson text, the site&rsquo;s own figures and its films are licensed under
      <a href={CC} rel="license noopener" target="_blank">Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0</a>
      (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0). This page says what that means in plain words. The licence itself is the
      full statement, and it wins if this page and the licence differ.
    </p>

    <h2 id="may">What you may do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Copy and share.</strong> Print a lesson, post it in your class site, or hand it out.</li>
      <li><strong>Adapt for teaching.</strong> Shorten it, translate it, mix it with your own material, or use a figure or a film in your slides.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Two conditions come with that:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Give credit.</strong> Say where it came from and say if you changed it. The next section shows how.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep the same licence.</strong> If you share your adapted version, share it under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 too.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>You do not need to ask first, and you do not need an account.</p>

    <h2 id="may-not">What you may not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sell it.</strong> You may not charge for the lessons, figures or films, or use them mainly to make money.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Teaching with them in a school or college class is fine. If you want a use this page does
      not allow, <a href="/contact">write to me</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="credit">How to credit</h2>
    <p>Use this line, with a link to the lesson you used:</p>
    <p class="lic-credit">Biochemistrypedia, Chris Deppmann, biochemistrypedia.com</p>
    <p>
      For example: &ldquo;From Biochemistrypedia, Chris Deppmann, biochemistrypedia.com/lessons/action-potential/,
      CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. Shortened for class.&rdquo;
    </p>

    <h2 id="not-covered">What the licence does not cover</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The scientist profiles.</strong> The profiles of scientists in the lessons are drawn from the book
        <em>The Molecule Hunters</em>. All rights to them are reserved. You may link to them and read them
        with a class. Ask before you copy or adapt them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The name and the logo.</strong> &ldquo;Biochemistrypedia&rdquo; and the site&rsquo;s logo are not
        licensed. Use the name to give credit, and do not use it or the logo in a way that suggests your version is this site.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The code.</strong> The site&rsquo;s code has its own licence. The
        <a href="https://github.com/deppmann/biochempedia/blob/main/LICENSE">LICENSE file</a> in the public
        repository puts the site, its components, its build scripts and its original simulations under the MIT
        licence. That file covers the code only.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Other people&rsquo;s material.</strong> Anything that comes from someone else keeps its own terms.
        That includes the 3D structures from RCSB&nbsp;PDB and PubChem, simulations from PhET Interactive
        Simulations (CC&nbsp;BY&nbsp;4.0), and videos and podcasts embedded from YouTube and Spotify.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="lic-meta">
